INT. PUBLIC LIBRARY - BOOK RETURNS ROOM - NIGHT

A back room behind the circulation desk. Steel carts piled with returned books. A conveyor belt chugs. A radiator ticks. The overhead light has a moth in it.

LEOPOLD (60s, cardigan, lanyard) shelves a cart, humming a waltz. WINIFRED (60s, volunteer vest, library-card earrings) peels stickers from paperbacks at a table. Neither looks up when the book-drop chute rattles and a thick novel thumps into the bin.

WINIFRED
That's the Dostoevsky again.

LEOPOLD
Fourth time this month.

WINIFRED
Somebody's reading it very slowly.

LEOPOLD
Somebody's very patient.

WINIFRED
Or in love.

LEOPOLD
With a Russian?

WINIFRED
Leopold, it happens.

He laughs, shakes his head. The chute rattles again. A paperback with a cracked spine tumbles out. Winifred flips it open. Something slides from the pages: a folded note.

WINIFRED (CONT'D)
Oh.

LEOPOLD
What?

WINIFRED
"Same time next Tuesday?"

LEOPOLD
Is it signed?

WINIFRED
It says "C."

LEOPOLD
Well, C will have to wait.

The back door squeaks. A STRANGER (30s, long coat, scarf wound to his chin, rain on his hair) leans in, hesitating.

STRANGER
Pardon me. I'm sorry. Is this where you leave things?

WINIFRED
The main desk closed at nine.

STRANGER
I wasn't looking for the desk.

LEOPOLD
Are you looking for a book, young man?

STRANGER
I'm looking for a note.

Winifred clutches the paper to her chest. Leopold tucks his hands behind his back. The stranger smiles, and it changes his whole face.

STRANGER (CONT'D)
My name is Caspian. I leave one in this one every Tuesday.

WINIFRED
For whom?

CASPIAN
For whoever finds it.

LEOPOLD
That seems unwise.

CASPIAN
It's led to some very good conversations.

WINIFRED
Leopold, he's the C.

LEOPOLD
I gathered.

The chute rattles one more time. A woman's voice floats down the shaft, laughing: "Caspian? Are you down there?" A pair of shoes appears at the lip of the opening, then a pair of legs, and AGNES (30s, red umbrella, wet laughing eyes) squeezes through and drops to the floor with a grunt.

AGNES
I told you the chute was big enough.

CASPIAN
It's a book drop, Agnes.

AGNES
It was a very comfortable book drop.

She notices the two older people. Straightens her coat. Holds out a hand.

AGNES (CONT'D)
Good evening. I'm so sorry about the intrusion.

LEOPOLD
Not at all.

WINIFRED
It's been a slow night.

AGNES
We'll just collect his note and be going.

WINIFRED
Of course.

She hands over the paper. Caspian takes it, unfolds it, reads it, and offers it to Agnes. She laughs, kisses his cheek, and tucks it into her pocket.

A third figure appears at the door, MURIEL (70s, librarian's brooch, stern as a verdict). The two lovers freeze. Leopold and Winifred straighten up.

MURIEL
It is eleven o'clock. This room is closed.

CASPIAN
We were just leaving.

MURIEL
By the door. Not the chute.

AGNES
Yes, ma'am.

They file out. Muriel watches them go. When they are gone, she looks at the note-less table, at Winifred's guilty face, at Leopold's studied innocence.

MURIEL
Which of you encouraged them?

LEOPOLD
I shelve. I don't encourage.

WINIFRED
Neither do I.

Muriel nods. Picks up the Dostoevsky. Opens it. Takes out a second note. Reads it. Her lips twitch.

MURIEL
Next Tuesday, then.
